"""Results, discussion and conclusions. Every number is read from Manuscript Numbers.json (N)."""
import math


def pp(x, d=1):
    return f"{x*100:.{d}f} percentage points"


def pct(x, d=1):
    return "n/a" if x is None else f"{100 * x:.{d}f}%"


def num(x, d=3):
    return "n/a" if x is None else f"{x:.{d}f}"


def results(N, S):
    """S holds sensitivity-derived numbers prepared by the builder."""
    B = []
    h1 = lambda t: B.append({"type": "h1", "text": t})
    h2 = lambda t: B.append({"type": "h2", "text": t})
    p = lambda t: B.append({"type": "p", "text": t})
    U = N["utility"]
    D = N["decisions"]

    def uv(task, model, k=0, d=3):
        v = U[task][model][k]
        return num(v, d)

    def dv(task, model, method, metric):
        return D.get(f"{task}|{model}|{method}|{metric}")

    h1("Results")
    h2("RQ1: privacy budgets and utility")
    p(f"Formal privacy reduced utility most where the target class was rare and least where the signal was strong or continuous («T:utility», «F:privacy_utility»). On AI4I maintenance the PR-AUC of FedAvg was {uv('maintenance|simulated','fedavg')} (95% interval {uv('maintenance|simulated','fedavg',1)} to {uv('maintenance|simulated','fedavg',2)}); DP-FedAvg reached {uv('maintenance|simulated','dp eps 8')} at ε = 8, {uv('maintenance|simulated','dp eps 4')} at ε = 4 and {uv('maintenance|simulated','dp eps 1')} at ε = 1, against a prevalence reference of {uv('maintenance|simulated','reference')}. The three Bosch designs followed the same pattern: with machine clients FedAvg reached {uv('bosch|machines','fedavg')} and DP-FedAvg {uv('bosch|machines','dp eps 8')}, {uv('bosch|machines','dp eps 4')} and {uv('bosch|machines','dp eps 1')} at ε = 8, 4 and 1. On HAI the corresponding values were {uv('hai|sessions','fedavg')}, {uv('hai|sessions','dp eps 8')}, {uv('hai|sessions','dp eps 4')} and {uv('hai|sessions','dp eps 1')}. Macro F1 for steel fell from {uv('steel|simulated','fedavg')} to {uv('steel|simulated','dp eps 8')}, {uv('steel|simulated','dp eps 4')} and {uv('steel|simulated','dp eps 1')}. Regression lost much less: power-plant RMSE moved from {uv('power|simulated','fedavg',0,2)} MW under FedAvg to {uv('power|simulated','dp eps 4',0,2)} MW at ε = 4 and {uv('power|simulated','dp eps 1',0,2)} MW at ε = 1, and gas-turbine RMSE from {uv('gas|simulated','fedavg',0,2)} to {uv('gas|simulated','dp eps 4',0,2)} and {uv('gas|simulated','dp eps 1',0,2)} mg/m^3^. RT-IoT2022, where attacks are the majority class, stayed close to ceiling (PR-AUC {uv('iot|simulated','fedavg')} without and {uv('iot|simulated','dp eps 1')} with ε = 1). SECOM was hard for every method: the best PR-AUC was {uv('semiconductor|simulated','linear')} for logistic regression against a reference of {uv('semiconductor|simulated','reference')}, and DP models were close to the reference.")
    p(f"The loss is concentrated in the minority class, as predicted by the disparate-impact result for DP-SGD {{bagdasaryan2019}}. Expressed as a share of FedAvg utility, DP-FedAvg at ε = 4 retained {pct(N['ratio_dp4_maintenance|simulated'],0)} on AI4I, {pct(N['ratio_dp4_bosch|machines'],0)} on Bosch machines, {pct(N['ratio_dp4_hai|sessions'],0)} on HAI and {pct(N['ratio_dp4_steel|simulated'],0)} on steel, while regression error rose by {pct(N['utility']['power|simulated']['dp eps 4'][0]/N['utility']['power|simulated']['fedavg'][0]-1,1)} for power and {pct(N['utility']['gas|simulated']['dp eps 4'][0]/N['utility']['gas|simulated']['fedavg'][0]-1,1)} for gas. The pooled network was the strongest neural model on AI4I ({uv('maintenance|simulated','central')}) and the linear model was competitive on Bosch and best on SECOM, so the gap between federated and pooled learning is task-specific and the linear reference remains necessary.")
    B.append({"type": "table_ref", "id": "utility"})
    B.append({"type": "figure_ref", "id": "privacy_utility"})
    h2("RQ2: heterogeneity, clients and machines")
    p(f"«F:models» compares all methods. Local-only models trained on one client's data underperformed the federated model on every simulated-client task except SECOM, where all neural models stayed near the reference; on AI4I the average local model reached a PR-AUC of {num(S['local_mean_maint'])} and the weakest {num(S['local_min_maint'])}, against {uv('maintenance|simulated','fedavg')} for FedAvg. The heterogeneity corrections did not improve on FedAvg consistently: FedProx was within {num(S['fedprox_gap_max'],3)} of FedAvg or below it on every task, and SCAFFOLD was best on Bosch machines ({uv('bosch|machines','scaffold')}) and HAI ({uv('hai|sessions','scaffold')}) but lower on AI4I ({uv('maintenance|simulated','scaffold')}), where it diverged at larger learning rates during tuning. {S['alpha_text']} Machine structure mattered more than simulated skew. A federation of two Bosch machines reached a PR-AUC of {num(S['unseen_pr_fedavg'])} on the unseen third machine against {uv('bosch|machine holdout','fedavg')} on held-out recordings from the two training machines, and later Bosch periods gave {num(S['later_pr_fedavg'])}, so most of the apparent accuracy does not transfer across machines or across years. HAI was the exception: later sessions gave a PR-AUC of {num(S['hai_later_pr_fedavg'])}, close to the within-session value.")
    B.append({"type": "figure_ref", "id": "models"})
    h2("RQ3: calibration, minority events and leakage")
    p(f"The default decision rule (pass below 0.5, alarm above) missed most faults once privacy was added. Under FedAvg its realised miss rate was {pct(dv('maintenance|simulated','fedavg','default','miss_rate'))} on AI4I, {pct(dv('bosch|machines','fedavg','default','miss_rate'))} on Bosch machines and {pct(dv('hai|sessions','fedavg','default','miss_rate'))} on HAI; under DP-FedAvg at ε = 4 it rose to {pct(dv('maintenance|simulated','dp eps 4','default','miss_rate'))}, {pct(dv('bosch|machines','dp eps 4','default','miss_rate'))} and {pct(dv('hai|sessions','dp eps 4','default','miss_rate'))}. Raw scores were miscalibrated where class weights were used (ECE {num(S['ece_fedavg_maint'])} for FedAvg on AI4I and {num(S['ece_fedavg_secom'])} on SECOM). The binned probabilities that come free with the private histograms reduced ECE to {num(S['binece_fedavg_maint'])} and {num(S['binece_fedavg_secom'])} respectively at no extra privacy cost; on tasks where the raw scores were already well calibrated the binned values were similar.")
    p(f"Membership inference by loss threshold was close to chance overall: attack AUC ranged from {num(N['mia_auc_range'][0])} to {num(N['mia_auc_range'][1])} across tasks and models, and the true positive rate at 1 per cent false positives stayed near 1 per cent. The rare class was different. Restricting the attack to fault records, the AUC for the non-private pooled model reached {num(N['mia'].get('semiconductor|simulated|central|auc_rare_class'))} on SECOM, {num(N['mia'].get('hai|sessions|central|auc_rare_class'))} on HAI and {num(N['mia'].get('bosch|machines|central|auc_rare_class'))} on Bosch machines, and for FedAvg {num(N['mia'].get('semiconductor|simulated|fedavg|auc_rare_class'))}, {num(N['mia'].get('hai|sessions|fedavg|auc_rare_class'))} and {num(N['mia'].get('bosch|machines|fedavg|auc_rare_class'))}; DP-FedAvg at ε = 4 brought these to {num(N['mia'].get('semiconductor|simulated|dp eps 4|auc_rare_class'))}, {num(N['mia'].get('hai|sessions|dp eps 4|auc_rare_class'))} and {num(N['mia'].get('bosch|machines|dp eps 4|auc_rare_class'))}. These rare-class estimates rest on 10 to 40 fault records per run and are therefore imprecise, but they locate the privacy risk where the industrial value lies: in the few recorded failures. {S['recon_text']}")
    h2("RQ4: validity and cost of private aggregate risk control")
    p(f"H1 was supported. In the Monte Carlo study ({N.get('mc_rows', 0)} conditions of 1,000 replications each) the largest violation rate of PARC across all conditions was {pct(N.get('mc_proposed_max_miss_violation'))} for the miss target and {pct(N.get('mc_proposed_max_fa_violation'))} for the false-alarm target, below the stated 5 per cent per side. The private plug-in rule violated the miss target in up to {pct(N.get('mc_plugin_max_miss_violation'))} of replications and the uncorrected binomial rule in up to {pct(N.get('mc_ucb_without_noise_max_miss_violation'))}, which shows that ignoring the privacy noise breaks the guarantee when calibration counts are small («F:reliability»).")
    h = N["hyp"]
    p(f"H2 was supported on the industrial data. Across {h['H2']['runs']} paired run-model combinations of the seven binary designs, the private plug-in rule exceeded the 10 per cent miss target on the held-out test rows in {h['H2']['plugin_exceedances']} cases and PARC in {h['H2']['proposed_exceedances']} (exact one-sided McNemar p = {h['H2']['p_one_sided_exact_mcnemar']:.1e}, Holm-adjusted p = {h['holm_adjusted']['H2']:.1e}). The non-private plug-in rule exceeded the target as often as its private version ({h['H2']['nonprivate_plugin_exceedances']} cases) and non-private Learn-then-Test as rarely as PARC ({h['H2']['nonprivate_ltt_exceedances']}). «F:ablation» shows the same ordering for false alarms: the share of runs above the 5 per cent target was {pct(N['fa_exceed_share']['plugin'])} for the private plug-in rule and {pct(N['fa_exceed_share']['proposed'])} for PARC. Test-set sampling contributes to these counts, so a run above the target does not by itself show that the population rate exceeded it. Because combinations within a run share one data partition, we also treated each of the {h['clustered_checks']['H2_clusters']} runs as a cluster: the plug-in rule exceeded the target more often than PARC in {h['clustered_checks']['H2_clusters_plugin_worse']} runs and less often in {h['clustered_checks']['H2_clusters_parc_worse'] or 'none'} (sign test p = {h['clustered_checks']['H2_cluster_sign_test_p']:.1e}).")
    p(f"H3 was supported in the form it was frozen, but the comparison turned out to say more about training than about calibration. At ε = 4 the escalation rate under PARC exceeded that of non-private Learn-then-Test on the same DP model by {pp(h['H3']['mean_difference'],2)} on average (95% interval {h['H3']['ci_low']*100:.2f} to {h['H3']['ci_high']*100:.2f}, {h['H3']['runs']} runs), far inside the five-point margin. Both rules escalated almost everything because the DP models placed too many faults at low scores for any automatic-pass region to be certified: escalation under PARC was {pct(dv('maintenance|simulated','dp eps 4','proposed','escalation_rate'))} on AI4I, {pct(dv('bosch|machines','dp eps 4','proposed','escalation_rate'))} on Bosch machines and {pct(dv('hai|sessions','dp eps 4','proposed','escalation_rate'))} on HAI. The added calibration-only sweep isolates the cost of private calibration on the non-private FedAvg model. {S['sweep_text']}")
    p(f"The evidence floor explains most escalation decisions. Certifying a 10 per cent miss rate requires at least {N['floor']['miss rate 0.10|non-private']} calibration faults without privacy, {N['floor']['miss rate 0.10|4']} at ε = 4, {N['floor']['miss rate 0.10|2']} at ε = 2 and {N['floor']['miss rate 0.10|1']} at ε = 1; certifying a 5 per cent false-alarm rate requires {N['floor']['false-alarm rate 0.05|non-private']}, {N['floor']['false-alarm rate 0.05|4']}, {N['floor']['false-alarm rate 0.05|2']} and {N['floor']['false-alarm rate 0.05|1']} normal records («T:floor»). The federations held on average {N['cal_pos']['maintenance|simulated']:.0f} calibration faults for AI4I, {N['cal_pos']['hai|sessions']:.0f} for HAI, {N['cal_pos']['semiconductor|simulated']:.0f} for SECOM and {N['cal_pos']['bosch|machines']:.0f}, {N['cal_pos']['bosch|time holdout']:.0f} and {N['cal_pos']['bosch|machine holdout']:.0f} for the three Bosch designs. SECOM and Bosch therefore sit below the floor at any budget and every certified method, private or not, escalated all faults there; AI4I and HAI clear it at ε = 4 or above. On AI4I with the FedAvg model, PARC automated {pct(1-dv('maintenance|simulated','fedavg','proposed','escalation_rate'))} of decisions with a realised miss rate of {pct(dv('maintenance|simulated','fedavg','proposed','miss_rate'))} and a false-alarm rate of {pct(dv('maintenance|simulated','fedavg','proposed','false_alarm_rate'))}; the private plug-in rule automated {pct(1-dv('maintenance|simulated','fedavg','plugin','escalation_rate'))} with a miss rate of {pct(dv('maintenance|simulated','fedavg','plugin','miss_rate'))}, at the target on average and above it in many runs. On RT-IoT2022, with more than 20,000 calibration attacks, all certified rules automated every decision with realised miss rates of {pct(dv('iot|simulated','fedavg','proposed','miss_rate'))} under FedAvg and {pct(dv('iot|simulated','dp eps 1','proposed','miss_rate'))} under DP at ε = 1.")
    p(f"For steel, selective classification under PARC accepted {pct(dv('steel|simulated','fedavg','proposed','coverage'))} of plates for the FedAvg model with a joint error of {pct(dv('steel|simulated','fedavg','proposed','joint_error'))} against a target of 10 per cent; the default rule accepted all plates with {pct(dv('steel|simulated','fedavg','default','joint_error'))} errors, and DP models at ε = 4 or below could not certify any acceptance region. Private prediction intervals for regression achieved in-period coverage of {pct(dv('power|simulated','fedavg','proposed','coverage'))} for power (half-width {num(dv('power|simulated','fedavg','proposed','half_width'),2)} MW) and {pct(dv('gas|simulated','fedavg','proposed','coverage'))} for gas (half-width {num(dv('gas|simulated','fedavg','proposed','half_width'),2)} mg/m^3^) at a 90 per cent target, with interval width growing as ε fell (power {num(dv('power|simulated','dp eps 1','proposed','half_width'),2)} MW at ε = 1).")
    B.append({"type": "table_ref", "id": "decisions"})
    B.append({"type": "table_ref", "id": "floor"})
    B.append({"type": "table_ref", "id": "tests"})
    B.append({"type": "figure_ref", "id": "ablation"})
    h2("RQ5: shift, unseen machines and attacks")
    hb = {x["shift"]: x["mean"] for x in h["H4"]["by_shift"]}
    p(f"H4 was supported overall (mean rise in realised risk {pp(h['H4']['mean_difference'])}, 95% interval {h['H4']['ci_low']*100:.1f} to {h['H4']['ci_high']*100:.1f}, {h['H4']['pairs']} pairs, Holm-adjusted p = {h['holm_adjusted']['H4']:.1e}) but the effect came from the gas turbine («F:shift»). Intervals certified on 2011 to 2013 had realised risk {pp(hb.get('year 2014'))} higher on the 2014 records and {pp(hb.get('year 2015'))} higher on the 2015 records than on in-period records, and recalibrating on 2014 with PARC restored coverage of 2015 only to {pct(N['gas_recal'].get('fedavg|proposed|coverage_2015'))} for FedAvg. RMSE rose from {uv('gas|simulated','fedavg',0,2)} mg/m^3^ in period to {num(S['gas_rmse_2015'],2)} in 2015. On Bosch and HAI the miss rate under PARC did not rise because the protocol had escalated every fault in the first place, so these designs do not test the guarantee under shift. They do show that escalation protects the miss rate when evidence is thin, at the price of human review. Averaging differences within runs ({h['clustered_checks']['H4_clusters']} clusters) gave a mean rise of {pp(h['clustered_checks']['H4_cluster_mean'])} (Wilcoxon p = {h['clustered_checks']['H4_cluster_wilcoxon_p']:.4f}) and without the gas turbine the mean rise was {pp(h['clustered_checks']['H4_without_gas_mean'])}.")
    p(f"On the later HAI sessions, all 17 attack events were flagged (alarm or escalation) by the FedAvg model under PARC and {pct(N['hai'].get('fedavg|proposed|detected_by_alarm'))} raised an automatic alarm, with a median delay to the first alarm of {num(N['hai'].get('fedavg|proposed|median_alarm_delay_seconds'),1)} s; {pct(N['hai'].get('fedavg|proposed|normal_windows_alarmed'))} of normal windows raised an alarm. With DP at ε = 4 automatic alarms covered {pct(N['hai'].get('dp eps 4|proposed|detected_by_alarm'))} of events with a median delay of {num(N['hai'].get('dp eps 4|proposed|median_alarm_delay_seconds'),1)} s. {S['attack_text']}")
    B.append({"type": "figure_ref", "id": "shift"})
    B.append({"type": "table_ref", "id": "robustness"})
    B.append({"type": "figure_ref", "id": "robustness"})
    h2("Privacy accounting and cost")
    pv = N["privacy"]
    p(f"Every DP model met its target: the largest per-client training budget was {num(pv['dp eps 4']['epsilon_training_max'][1])} at the ε = 4 setting and {num(pv['dp eps 1']['epsilon_training_max'][1])} at ε = 1 («T:privacy»). The Skellam parameter needed for ε~C~ = ε was μ = {num(pv['dp eps 1']['skellam_mu_total'][0],2)} at ε = 1, {num(pv['dp eps 2']['skellam_mu_total'][0],2)} at ε = 2 and {num(pv['dp eps 4']['skellam_mu_total'][0],3)} at ε = 4. Because roles are assigned record by record, the pipeline budget equals the training budget; if the same records served both roles, sequential composition would give up to {num(pv['dp eps 4']['epsilon_total_sequential_worst_client'][1],2)} at the ε = 4 setting and {num(pv['dp eps 1']['epsilon_total_sequential_worst_client'][1],2)} at ε = 1. Without secure aggregation each client's own noise share would give its calibration records only ε = {num(pv['dp eps 4']['epsilon_calibration_without_secure_aggregation'][1],2)} instead of 4, and the same value applies if all other clients collude with the server. Costs were small at this model size («T:costs»): one DP-FedAvg training run took {num(S['dp_seconds_maint'],2)} s on AI4I and {num(S['dp_seconds_iot'],2)} s on RT-IoT2022 on one core, secure aggregation of model updates added {num(S['secagg_seconds_maint'],2)} s and {S['secagg_extra_bytes']} bytes of key exchange per training run, and the calibration release sent {S['cal_bytes_maint']:,} bytes in total. These are in-process measurements, not network measurements.")
    B.append({"type": "table_ref", "id": "privacy"})
    B.append({"type": "table_ref", "id": "costs"})
    B.append({"type": "figure_ref", "id": "reliability"})
    return B


def discussion(N, S):
    B = []
    h1 = lambda t: B.append({"type": "h1", "text": t})
    h2 = lambda t: B.append({"type": "h2", "text": t})
    p = lambda t: B.append({"type": "p", "text": t})
    h1("Discussion")
    p("The answer to the opening question is a qualified yes. Industrial sites can learn together under formal record-level privacy and can certify where automated decisions stay within stated error rates, with calibration that never exposes an unprotected statistic to the server. Whether that certification leaves much for automation depends on two quantities that are easy to measure before deployment: how well the shared model ranks rare events and how many rare events the sites hold for calibration. The privacy mechanism changes both, and on rare-fault tasks the training cost of privacy was the larger effect.")
    h2("What the findings establish")
    p(f"First, guarantees are achievable and cheap where evidence is adequate. The Monte Carlo study and {N['hyp']['H2']['runs']} paired industrial runs show that a noise-aware certificate keeps the miss rate within target where plug-in thresholds do not, and the price of private calibration is small once a site holds well above the evidence floor («F:reliability»). This matches the logic of Learn-then-Test {{angelopoulos2021ltt}} and private prediction sets {{angelopoulos2022private}} and extends it to distributed noise under secure aggregation {{agarwal2021,kairouz2021ddg}}. Second, the binding constraint for rare industrial faults is the count of calibration events. The floor of 31 faults for a 10 per cent miss target exists without any privacy; privacy at ε = 1 raises it to 118. Bosch and SECOM fell below it, and the correct response of a certified system was to escalate. Third, DP training at budgets common in the literature weakened rare-event ranking enough that certification failed even when calibration was exact, consistent with the disparate impact of DP-SGD {{bagdasaryan2019}}. Fourth, the guarantee is exchangeability-bound: drift in the gas turbine broke interval coverage and recalibration on the latest year did not repair it, so certified thresholds have a shelf life that must be managed.")
    h2("Mechanism: why the method succeeds or fails")
    p(f"PARC succeeds when three conditions hold together: the shared model separates faults from normal operation in the region where automation is wanted, the calibration set holds enough events for the target, and future data resemble calibration data. It fails safely when the first two conditions fail, because the scan stops and work moves to people. It fails unsafely when the third fails, because nothing in the certificate detects shift. The plug-in rule fails in a different way: it treats the empirical rate as the true rate, so its realised rate sits near the target on average and above it in a large share of runs ({pct(N['exceed_share']['plugin'],0)} of industrial runs and up to {pct(N['mc_plugin_max_miss_violation'])} of Monte Carlo replications); adding privacy noise does not correct this. The uncorrected binomial rule shows a subtler failure; it is valid when counts are large but breaks for small counts once noise is added, which is exactly the rare-fault regime.")
    h2("Industrial and managerial implications")
    p(f"Four operating rules follow. Sites should count calibration events before choosing a privacy budget: with fewer than about 36 faults per target in the pooled calibration set, a 10 per cent miss guarantee at ε = 4 cannot be certified and the federation should plan for full human review or relax the target (a 20 per cent target needs {N['floor']['miss rate 0.20|4']} faults at ε = 4). Privacy should be spent on training only where ranking survives it; on our rare-fault tasks, ε = 8 retained between half and four fifths of FedAvg's primary metric and ε = 1 retained little, whereas regression and majority-class detection kept most of their utility. Calibration should be private by default, since at ε~C~ of 4 or more it added almost no escalation on AI4I and HAI once evidence was adequate and it removes the server's view of per-site error statistics. Certificates should be dated and renewed: a governance process that recalibrates on recent data and monitors realised error is required, and drift as large as the 2014 to 2015 gas-turbine change needs model retraining as well as recalibration. Escalation is the human-oversight channel required for high-risk systems by Article 14 of the AI Act {{euaiact2024}} and recommended by the NIST AI RMF {{nist2023}}; PARC makes its size measurable and attaches an error bound to the automated remainder.")
    h2("What remains at risk after privacy is added")
    p(f"Differential privacy bounded membership inference on the rare class in our runs, but it did not protect against integrity attacks or drift. {S['discussion_attack_text']} Secure aggregation hides individual updates and calibration counts from the server, which also stops the server from screening them, so robust aggregation and input validation cannot be applied directly {{kairouz2021open,fang2020}}. Range proofs on each client's counts or a bounded per-client contribution would close the calibration channel; we did not implement them. Formal privacy also does not cover the normalisation statistics, which we treated as public, or hyperparameter tuning {{papernot2022}}.")
    h2("Implications for the Industry 6.0 discussion")
    p("Sources that use the Industry 6.0 label share an expectation that distributed, AI-driven control will cross organisational boundaries and that people will supervise automated judgement rather than operate machines {duggal2022,fernandezmiguel2025,lykov2025,babkin2025}. Our evidence speaks to a precondition for that expectation and does not show that it is being met. Collaborative learning across sites can be made private with measurable cost, and automated decisions can be bounded by certified error rates. The same evidence shows where the expectation is weakest: rare and high-consequence events, small data per site, and drift. Under those conditions the certified system returns most decisions to people. An Industry 6.0 that keeps the human-centred commitments of Industry 5.0 {breque2021,ivanov2023} therefore depends on sites pooling enough evidence about failures, which is a data-governance problem as well as a technical one.")
    B.append({"type": "table_ref", "id": "implications"})
    h2("Limitations")
    p("The federation in six tasks is simulated by label skew; only Bosch machines and HAI sessions supply natural partitions, and neither represents independent companies. AI4I is synthetic and RT-IoT2022 and HAI are testbeds. Calibration and test rows come from the same collection period except in the shift designs, so in-distribution guarantees may be optimistic for deployments. The 2015 gas-turbine year was inspected in an earlier pilot. Seed-level intervals do not measure variation across sites. The neural models are small; larger models may rank rare events better or memorise them more. DP-SGD was implemented in NumPy because Opacus was unavailable; its accounting used an established library and its mechanics are unit-tested, but it has not been audited externally. The membership attack is a simple loss threshold and the rare-class estimates rest on few records; stronger attacks would likely report higher leakage {carlini2021}. Secure aggregation was executed in one process without dropout recovery and with an honest-but-curious server. The poisoning experiments use one fixed attack. Hyperparameter tuning and normalisation are outside the privacy accounting. Several candidate datasets could not be acquired from the execution environment. Finally, the pilot results recorded earlier in the repository could not be re-verified because their workflow artefacts were not accessible, and they are not used here.")
    h1("Conclusions")
    p("Private aggregate risk control lets industrial sites certify, from calibration data protected by client-side Skellam noise and secure aggregation, which automated pass and alarm decisions stay within stated miss and false-alarm rates. The certificate was valid in simulation and on industrial data, and private calibration cost little where calibration evidence was adequate. Rare-fault tasks exposed the real limits: DP training weakened ranking of the events that matter most, and the number of recorded faults set a hard floor on what any certified method can automate. Practitioners should measure evidence before choosing budgets, keep calibration private, renew certificates as data drift and treat escalation as the designed channel for human oversight. Future work should test the protocol across independent organisations, add verifiable bounds on client contributions and study shift-aware certification.")
    return B
